        <span class="blog-tag chip chip--green">Pre-IB Mathematics</span>
        <h1>Pre-IB Maths to IB Diploma: AA or AI, HL or SL</h1>
        <div class="blog-post-meta">
          <a class="blog-author" href="/about/" rel="author">By Jeremy Lim, Founder</a>
          <span>&middot;</span>
          <time datetime="2026-09-25">25 September 2026</time>
          <span>&middot;</span>
          <span class="blog-reading-time">7 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-post-body">
        <p>If your child is in Year 3 or Year 4 of an Integrated Programme that leads to the IB Diploma, a maths decision is coming. In Year 5 they take one of four courses: Mathematics: analysis and approaches (AA) or Mathematics: applications and interpretation (AI), each at standard level (SL) or higher level (HL). The IB allows only one maths course in the Diploma, and some university courses accept only one of the four.</p>
        <p>This post sets out what the IB and four Singapore universities publish, and what your child can do in Year 3 and Year 4 to keep their options open. We checked every source on 25 September 2026. If you&rsquo;re still weighing the IB against the A-Levels, start with <a href="/blog/ib-vs-jc/">IB or JC?</a></p>

        <h2>Two courses, two levels</h2>
        <p>The IB recommends 150 teaching hours at SL and 240 at HL for both courses. Each topic starts with content the two courses share, and then they split.</p>
        <p><strong>Analysis and approaches</strong> is the more algebraic course. The IB&rsquo;s guide says it has &ldquo;a strong emphasis on the ability to construct, communicate and justify correct mathematical arguments&rdquo;, and that HL students &ldquo;will have strong algebraic skills and the ability to understand simple proof&rdquo;. It covers functions, trigonometry and calculus, plus sequences and series, with proof by induction at HL.</p>
        <p><strong>Applications and interpretation</strong> is built around modelling and statistics. The IB describes it as the course for students who like to see maths used in a practical context, and says it &ldquo;makes extensive use of technology&rdquo;. It also includes calculus and statistics.</p>
        <p>All four courses expect students to be fluent with a graphic display calculator. The difference is when they&rsquo;re allowed to use it.</p>

        <h2>How each course is assessed</h2>
        <table class="table-stack">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Course detail</th><th scope="col">AA SL</th><th scope="col">AA HL</th><th scope="col">AI SL</th><th scope="col">AI HL</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="Course detail">Recommended teaching hours</td><td data-label="AA SL">150</td><td data-label="AA HL">240</td><td data-label="AI SL">150</td><td data-label="AI HL">240</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Course detail">Written papers</td><td data-label="AA SL">Papers 1 and 2, 90 min each</td><td data-label="AA HL">Papers 1 and 2 (2 h each), Paper 3</td><td data-label="AI SL">Papers 1 and 2, 90 min each</td><td data-label="AI HL">Papers 1 and 2 (2 h each), Paper 3</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Course detail">Calculator</td><td data-label="AA SL">None in Paper 1; required in Paper 2</td><td data-label="AA HL">None in Paper 1; required in Papers 2 and 3</td><td data-label="AI SL">Required in both papers</td><td data-label="AI HL">Required in all three papers</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Course detail">Exam weighting</td><td data-label="AA SL">40% + 40%</td><td data-label="AA HL">30% + 30% + 20%</td><td data-label="AI SL">40% + 40%</td><td data-label="AI HL">30% + 30% + 20%</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Course detail">Internal assessment (the exploration)</td><td data-label="AA SL">20%</td><td data-label="AA HL">20%</td><td data-label="AI SL">20%</td><td data-label="AI HL">20%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Source: the IB&rsquo;s subject guides for the current courses (first assessment 2021).</p>
        <p>Two things stand out. In AA, a paper with no calculator carries 40% of the SL grade and 30% of the HL grade, so algebra done by hand counts for a lot. In AI, a calculator is required on every paper.</p>
        <p>The exploration is the same task in all four courses: an individual piece of written work investigating an area of maths, marked out of 20 by the teacher and moderated by the IB. The guides allow up to 15 hours of class time for it.</p>

        <h2>A new syllabus starts in 2027</h2>
        <p>The IB will teach new versions of both courses from 2027, first examined in 2029. The current courses are examined for the last time in November 2028. So a student in Year 4 in 2026, who finishes the Diploma in 2028, takes the current courses. A student in Year 3 in 2026 finishes in 2029 and takes the new ones.</p>
        <p>The IB has published the changes to AI. Content is reduced and nothing is added. AI SL drops logarithms and the trapezoidal rule. AI HL drops complex numbers, the vector product, the Poisson distribution and three hypothesis tests. SL papers fall from 80 to 75 marks, HL Papers 1 and 2 from 110 to 100, and HL Paper 3 from 55 marks and 75 minutes to 50 marks and one hour. The exploration stays, with the same marking criteria at SL and HL. Ask your child&rsquo;s school which version its Year 5 cohort will follow.</p>

        <h2>What the universities ask for</h2>
        <p>Prerequisites change every year, and NUS says so on its prerequisites page. The lists below are the ones published for 2026 admission, so check the current list when your child applies. Universities write the courses as MAA and MAI.</p>
        <p><strong>NUS</strong> (list accurate as of December 2025) names the course as well as the level:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Engineering, Computer Engineering and Business Analytics: a pass in HL AA.</li>
          <li>Data Science and Economics: a very good pass in HL AA.</li>
          <li>Computer Science programmes: a pass in HL Computer Science, HL AA or HL Physics, or a good pass in SL AA or HL AI.</li>
          <li>Business Administration: a pass in HL AA, SL AA or HL AI.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Across NUS&rsquo;s list, HL AI is accepted wherever SL AA is, and SL AI doesn&rsquo;t appear as meeting any maths prerequisite. NUS&rsquo;s College of Humanities and Sciences names HL AA as the equivalent of H2 Mathematics. Its Economics major has no subject prerequisite, while majors that ask for H2 Mathematics, such as Quantitative Finance, send students without it to a bridging course (MA1301).</p>
        <p><strong>NTU</strong> (correct as at February 2026) accepts either course and states there is &ldquo;no preference of MAA or MAI over the other&rdquo;. The level is what counts. Its engineering programmes need Maths at HL and a science at HL. Data Science and Artificial Intelligence and Mathematical Sciences need Maths at HL. Economics needs a good grade in Maths at SL, and Business and Accountancy need Maths at SL.</p>
        <p><strong>SMU</strong> (AY2026&ndash;27) asks Economics and Computer Science applicants for &ldquo;a good pass in IB Diploma Math at Higher Level or Standard Level&rdquo;. Applicants without it can still be considered if they have other maths background.</p>
        <p><strong>SUTD</strong> recommends HL Maths, either AA or AI, plus at least one HL science such as Physics or Chemistry. It also takes Year 4 grades into account.</p>
        <p>In short: for engineering, computing or data science at NUS, plan on HL AA. At NTU and SUTD, the level matters more than the course. HL AA meets the maths part of every requirement in these four lists, subject to the grade asked for.</p>

        <h2>Check what your school offers</h2>
        <p>Not every school runs all four courses. ACS (Independent)&rsquo;s Diploma subject list, updated 7 September 2026, offers Mathematics: analysis and approaches at HL and SL only, and says a class forms only if enough students choose a subject. At a school like that, the decision is SL or HL. Read your school&rsquo;s list before you plan around AI.</p>

        <h2>What matters most in Year 3 and Year 4</h2>
        <p>Both IB guides list the &ldquo;prior learning&rdquo; students should know before the course, and say exam questions assume it. Five items are marked HL only: highest common factor and lowest common multiple, rational exponents, rationalising the denominator, quadratic equations and inequalities, and adding and subtracting algebraic fractions. These are the indices, surds and algebra a Pre-IB course covers.</p>
        <p>Three topics show where the courses part ways:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Logarithms.</strong> AA SL includes the laws of logarithms. In AI they&rsquo;re HL only, and the new AI SL removes logarithms altogether.</li>
          <li><strong>Differentiation.</strong> AA SL students use the chain rule. In AI, the chain, product and quotient rules are HL only.</li>
          <li><strong>First principles.</strong> Of the four courses, only AA HL defines the derivative from first principles.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Aim to have all of this secure, without a calculator, by the end of Year 4.</p>

        <h2>How to decide</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Start from the degree.</strong> List two or three university courses your child is considering and read this year&rsquo;s IB prerequisites for each. If one says HL AA, the choice is made.</li>
          <li><strong>Look at how marks are lost.</strong> Go through Year 4 test papers and mark where each error happened. Algebra slips and skipped steps are exactly what AA&rsquo;s no-calculator Paper 1 tests.</li>
          <li><strong>Match the course to how your child likes to work.</strong> Proof and algebraic argument point to AA. Data, modelling and calculator work point to AI.</li>
          <li><strong>Weigh the hours.</strong> HL maths is 240 hours against 150 at SL, and it takes up one of your child&rsquo;s HL subjects.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>What to do in Year 4</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Redo past school tests without a calculator, writing every line of working.</li>
          <li>Practise the HL-only prior learning until it&rsquo;s automatic: rational exponents, rationalising denominators and algebraic fractions.</li>
          <li>Learn the laws of logarithms and use them to solve exponential equations by hand.</li>
          <li>Differentiate simple polynomials from first principles, then check the answers with the rules.</li>
          <li>Find your school&rsquo;s subject list and choice deadline, and read the NUS, NTU, SMU and SUTD prerequisite pages for the courses your child is considering.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How we help</h2>
        <p>Our <a href="/pre-ib-mathematics/">Pre-IB Mathematics (Year 3 &amp; Year 4)</a> classes cover indices, surds, algebra, logarithms, trigonometry and an introduction to calculus, including differentiation from first principles and integration. Lessons are online, 90 minutes a week, in groups of at most 6. We teach with the SHARP Method (See, Hit, Apply, Refine, Practise) and full, line-by-line working.</p>

